              {
                "text": "The command-line program `src/expand.py` has been implemented to expand compact range lists from stdin, supporting both ascending and descending ranges, as well as negative numbers.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef parse_range(item):\n    \"\"\"Parses a range string 'a-b' into integers a and b, handling negative numbers.\"\"\"\n    parts = item.split('-')\n    if len(parts) == 2:\n        return int(parts[0]), int(parts[1])\n    elif len(parts) == 3:\n        # One of the numbers is negative\n        if parts[0] == '':\n            # First number is negative: -a-b\n            return -int(parts[1]), int(parts[2])\n        else:\n            # Second number is negative: a--b\n            return int(parts[0]), -int(parts[2])\n    elif len(parts) == 4:\n        # Both numbers are negative: -a--b\n        return -int(parts[1]), -int(parts[3])\n    else:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid range format\")\n\ndef expand_ranges():\n    try:\n        input_data = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n        if not input_data:\n            return\n\n        items = input_data.split(',')\n        result = []\n\n        for item in items:\n            item = item.strip()\n            if not item:\n                continue\n            \n            if '-' in item:\n                try:\n                    start, end = parse_range(item)\n                    \n                    if start <= end:\n                        result.extend(range(start, end + 1))\n                    else:\n                        result.extend(range(start, end - 1, -1))\n                except ValueError:\n                    pass\n            else:\n                try:\n                    result.append(int(item))\n                except ValueError:\n                    pass\n        \n        print(*(result))\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    expand_ranges()\n```",
